composed prompt\" helper, used by `entire review --fix` and\n// `entire investigate fix`. Both commands feed accepted findings back into\n// a follow-up coding agent without spawning a review/investigate session\n// themselves.\npackage agentlaunch\n\nimport (\n    \"context\"\n)\n\n// LaunchFixAgent starts a normal coding agent session with the given\n// prompt. ENTIRE_REVIEW_* and ENTIRE_INVESTIGATE_* env entries are stripped\n// from the child process so the fix session is not tagged as a review or\n// investigate.\n//\n// agentName must be a launchable agent registry name. Returns nil on clean\n// exit, or a wrapped error on cancellation / non-zero exit. Output / input\n// are connected to the calling process's stdio so the user can interact\n// with the fix session in their terminal.\nfunc LaunchFixAgent(ctx context.Context, agentName string, prompt string) error\n```\n\nThe implementation:\n1. `agent.Get(types.AgentName(agentName))` to resolve the agent.\n2. `agent.LauncherFor(ag.Name())` to get the interactive launcher (the one used today by `launchReviewFixAgent`).\n3. `launcher.LaunchCmd(ctx, prompt)` for the `*exec.Cmd`.\n4. Set `cmd.Stdin = os.Stdin`, `cmd.Stdout = os.Stdout`, `cmd.Stderr = os.Stderr` so the user sees the agent. Look at the current `launchReviewFixAgent` body to confirm this is what it does (it relies on `LaunchCmd` to set stdio, but verify).\n5. Strip env: `cmd.Env = withoutReviewOrInvestigateEnv(cmd.Env)` then if `cmd.Env` is empty, fall back to `withoutReviewOrInvestigateEnv(os.Environ())` (mirror the existing pattern).\n6. Run; wrap errors as today (`context.Canceled` → \"fix agent cancelled\", `*exec.ExitError` → \"fix agent exited with status %d\", else \"run fix agent: %w\").\n\nThe strip helper:\n```go\n// withoutReviewOrInvestigateEnv returns a copy of base with all\n// ENTIRE_REVIEW_* and ENTIRE_INVESTIGATE_* entries removed.\nfunc withoutReviewOrInvestigateEnv(base []string) []string {\n    out := make([]string, 0, len(base))\n    for _, kv := range base {\n        if review.IsReviewEnvEntry(kv) || investigate.IsInvestigateEnvEntry(kv) {\n            continue\n        }\n        out = append(out, kv)\n    }\n    return out\n}\n```\n\n**Note:** `review.IsReviewEnvEntry` is currently lower-case `isReviewEnvEntry` (unexported, in `cmd/entire/cli/review/env.go`). You will need to either:\n- Export it as `IsReviewEnvEntry`, OR\n- Replicate the prefix-match locally in `agentlaunch/launch.go` against the 5 review env names.\n\n**Choose: export it from review.** The existing `IsInvestigateEnvEntry` is already exported in investigate, and consistency wins over hiding internals. Update `cmd/entire/cli/review/env.go` so `isReviewEnvEntry` becomes `IsReviewEnvEntry` (uppercase, exported). Update the one in-package caller in `review/fix.go` (`withoutReviewEnv`) too — it currently calls `isReviewEnvEntry`, just rename the call to `IsReviewEnvEntry`.\n\nAdd tests in `cmd/entire/cli/agentlaunch/launch_test.go`:\n- `TestWithoutReviewOrInvestigateEnv` — table-driven: pass a slice with mixed entries (ENTIRE_REVIEW_*, ENTIRE_INVESTIGATE_*, normal vars), assert both env families are removed and normal vars survive.\n- For the `LaunchFixAgent` end-to-end behavior, you'd need a fake agent — that's exercised by the integration tests in Task 11. A minimal unit test that patches the launcher is fine if you can make it work without too much yak-shaving; otherwise, `TestWithoutReviewOrInvestigateEnv` alone is enough for this task.\n\n### 2. Update `cmd/entire/cli/review/fix.go` to call into `agentlaunch`\n\nReplace the body of `launchReviewFixAgent` with a one-liner:\n```go\nfunc launchReviewFixAgent(ctx context.Context, agentName string, prompt string) error {\n    return agentlaunch.LaunchFixAgent(ctx, agentName, prompt)\n}\n```\n\nOR delete `launchReviewFixAgent` entirely and update the single caller (`runReviewFix` in `review/fix.go` line ~108) to call `agentlaunch.LaunchFixAgent` directly. **Prefer the direct-call refactor** — fewer indirections.\n\nThe local `withoutReviewEnv` helper is no longer needed by `review/fix.go` after this change. If nothing else uses it, delete it. If something else does (search the package), leave it alone.\n\n### 3. Create `cmd/entire/cli/investigate/fix.go`\n\nThis is the `entire investigate fix [<run-id>]` subcommand.\n\nPublic API:\n\n```go\n// FixDeps collects what RunFix needs that's injectable for tests.\ntype FixDeps struct {\n    // ManifestStore loads local manifests by run ID.\n    ManifestStore *LocalManifestStore\n\n    // FixAgent is the agent registry name to launch. When empty, use a\n    // sensible default (e.g. settings.ReviewFixAgent or alphabetically\n    // first launchable; for now: hard-code \"claude-code\" with a TODO note\n    // so the cobra wiring task can layer on an --agent flag later).\n    FixAgent string\n\n    // Launch runs the actual coding agent session. Production wires this\n    // to agentlaunch.LaunchFixAgent. Tests inject a stub.\n    Launch func(ctx context.Context, agentName string, prompt string) error\n\n    // ReadFile, when non-nil, replaces os.ReadFile (test injection).\n    ReadFile func(name string) ([]byte, error)\n}\n\n// FixInput drives RunFix.\ntype FixInput struct {\n    // RunID resolves a specific run; empty means \"pick the most recent\".\n    RunID string\n\n    // Out, ErrOut are the user-facing streams for prompts/banners.\n    Out, ErrOut io.Writer\n}\n\n// RunFix resolves a saved investigation, composes the follow-up prompt,\n// and launches a coding agent session via deps.Launch.\n//\n// The prompt body says \"use these findings as grounded context, do not\n// re-investigate\". The composed prompt embeds the findings doc + timeline\n// doc bodies verbatim (so the agent has full access).\nfunc RunFix(ctx context.Context, in FixInput, deps FixDeps) error\n```\n\nAlgorithm:\n1. Resolve manifest:\n   - If `in.RunID != \"\"`, call `deps.ManifestStore.FindByRunID(ctx, in.RunID)`. If not found, return error \"no investigation found with run id %q\".\n   - Else, call `deps.ManifestStore.List(ctx)` and pick `manifests[0]` (List returns newest first). If empty, return \"no local investigations found\".\n2. Read findings + timeline bodies from disk: `os.ReadFile(manifest.FindingsDoc)` and `os.ReadFile(manifest.TimelineDoc)`. Use `deps.ReadFile` if non-nil for test injection. Tolerate missing files (warn but proceed with whatever is available — at least one of the docs should exist).\n3. Compose the follow-up prompt (port the format from the plan's §10):\n   ```\n   A prior multi-agent investigation produced these findings. Use them as\n   grounded context to plan the next step. Do not re-investigate the same\n   question — assume the findings are correct unless you find direct\n   evidence to the contrary.\n\n   Topic: <topic>\n   Run ID: <run-id>\n\n   ## Investigation findings\n\n   <findings doc body>\n\n   ## Investigation timeline\n\n   <timeline doc body>\n   ```\n4. Print a one-line banner to `in.Out`: `\"Launching <fix-agent> with findings from run <run-id> ...\"`.\n5. Call `deps.Launch(ctx, deps.FixAgent, prompt)`.\n\n### 4. Tests in `cmd/entire/cli/investigate/fix_test.go`\n\n- `TestRunFix_PicksMostRecent` — store has 2 manifests; RunID empty; assert deps.Launch called with prompt that contains the most-recent topic.\n- `TestRunFix_ResolvesByRunID` — RunID set; assert correct manifest selected.\n- `TestRunFix_RunIDNotFound` — RunID set, no match; expect error.\n- `TestRunFix_NoManifests` — empty store; expect \"no local investigations found\".\n- `TestRunFix_ComposesPromptBody` — pin a snippet of the expected prompt body (use a small fixture findings + timeline; assert prompt contains both the \"do not re-investigate\" line and the doc bodies verbatim).\n- `TestRunFix_TolerateMissingDocs` — manifest references files that don't exist; verify no error, prompt body has empty doc sections (or \"no findings recorded\" placeholder).\n\nUse `LocalManifestStore` with `NewLocalManifestStoreWithDir(t.TempDir())` and write fixtures via `Write`. Stub `deps.Launch` to capture (agentName, prompt) and `deps.ReadFile` if needed.\n\n## Files\n\nYou will create:\n- `cmd/entire/cli/agentlaunch/launch.go`\n- `cmd/entire/cli/agentlaunch/launch_test.go`\n- `cmd/entire/cli/investigate/fix.go`\n- `cmd/entire/cli/investigate/fix_test.go`\n\nYou will modify:\n- `cmd/entire/cli/review/env.go` — rename `isReviewEnvEntry` → `IsReviewEnvEntry`.\n- `cmd/entire/cli/review/fix.go` — drop `launchReviewFixAgent` body, call `agentlaunch.LaunchFixAgent` directly. Drop `withoutReviewEnv` helper if it's no longer used in the package (search `withoutReviewEnv` to confirm).\n\n## Constraints\n\n- Read CLAUDE.md before starting.\n- DO NOT modify CLAUDE.md or commit anything.\n- Tests must call `t.Parallel()`.\n- The cobra wiring (turning `RunFix` into an `entire investigate fix` cobra subcommand) is a later task — DO NOT add cobra glue here. This task's `RunFix` is a plain function; Task 5 (Cobra wiring) will wrap it.\n- Don't import cli package or any cli-package-only helpers from agentlaunch — agentlaunch is a leaf package consumed by both review and investigate.\n\n## Verification\n\n```bash\nmise run fmt && mise run lint && mise run test -- ./cmd/entire/cli/agentlaunch/... ./cmd/entire/cli/investigate/... ./cmd/entire/cli/review/...\n```\n\nThe existing review tests must still pass (the rename `isReviewEnvEntry` → `IsReviewEnvEntry` and the body swap to call agentlaunch).\n\n## Reporting\n\nReport DONE / DONE_WITH_CONCERNS / BLOCKED / NEEDS_CONTEXT. List files added/modified, tests added, verification output, concerns.","agentId":"aefa42a480bcc0cc5","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Files I created/modified per task scope:\n\nCreated:\n- `cmd/entire/cli/agentlaunch/launch.go`\n- `cmd/entire/cli/agentlaunch/launch_test.go`\n- `cmd/entire/cli/investigate/fix.go`\n- `cmd/entire/cli/investigate/fix_test.go`\n\nMo