t the child's `ENTIRE_REVIEW_*` would only be stripped from THIS child's spawn — not from any further children. That's actually fine here. But the bigger nit: tests confirm strip-on-spawn but no test asserts that a stale `ENTIRE_REVIEW_SESSION=1` in the parent env doesn't leak through. Add a test in `env_test.go` that calls `AppendInvestigateEnv` with both prefixes pre-set and asserts neither leaks.\n\n- **`cmd/entire/cli/investigate/loop.go:309` — per-turn log filename uses `state.Turn` only.** On `--continue`, if the user ran two concurrent `entire investigate --continue <id>` from different shells (rare but possible), they'd race on the same `turn-N-<agent>.log` file via `O_TRUNC`. The `RunState` Save uses `os.CreateTemp`+rename for safety; the per-turn log doesn't. Not a correctness issue (concurrent same-run resume is user error), but worth a comment or a `flock` to make the failure mode explicit. At minimum, document \"concurrent --continue on the same run id is unsupported.\"\n\n- **`cmd/entire/cli/investigate/loop.go:336` — `ParseStanceFromTimeline` ignores its `_` (the `found` bool).** When the agent didn't write a heading at all, `stance=\"unknown\"` and `note=\"timeline missing stance\"` — but `runOneTurn` doesn't distinguish \"agent ran cleanly but never wrote a heading\" from \"agent wrote heading but no stance line.\" Both yield identical TurnStance. Surface the missing-heading case as a different failure signal so consecutive missing-heading turns can trip pause-on-failure. Today, an agent that exits 0 but writes nothing burns the turn budget silently.\n\n- **`cmd/entire/cli/lifecycle.go:1218-1223` — `adoptInvestigateEnv` writes 5 fields without nil/empty validation.** If `EnvRunID` is empty (someone set `ENTIRE_INVESTIGATE_SESSION=1` manually), state is tagged `KindAgentInvestigate` with empty `InvestigateRunID` — downstream condensation would join with no run id. Reject with a warn-and-skip when `EnvRunID == \"\"` or fails `validateRunID`.\n\n- **`cmd/entire/cli/investigate/issuelink.go:135` — error message leaks `rawURL` into wrapped error.** Generally fine, but the URL could embed an OAuth token (`REDACTED`). Strip user-info from the URL before logging/returning. `u.Redacted()` is the stdlib helper.\n\n### Suggestions\n\n- `cmd/entire/cli/investigate/loop.go:632-648` — the `slogString`/`sRun`/`sErr` wrappers add 16 lines for marginal call-site savings. Consider deleting them and writing `slog.String(\"run_id\", id)` inline.\n- `cmd/entire/cli/agentlaunch/launch.go:42-53` and `cmd/entire/cli/investigate/env.go:79-85` — both files keep a duplicate list of `ENTIRE_REVIEW_*` names with a comment \"must be updated to match.\" This is the exact drift the spawner refactor was meant to avoid. Consider exporting the prefix list from a tiny `provenance` package both can import.\n- `cmd/entire/cli/investigate/loop.go:444-446` — `updateRoundCounter` recomputes a value that's already implicit in `state.Turn`. Either drop `state.Round` entirely or document that it's a denormalized cache for `entire investigate status`.\n- `cmd/entire/cli/investigate/cmd.go:341` — `Resuming investigation: %s (run %s)` prints `state.Topic`, which is user-supplied. If a topic contains ANSI escape codes and stdout is a TTY, this renders. Pass through a sanitizer or `%q`.\n\n### Praise\n\n- Spawner extraction is clean — `Spawner` interface owns argv only, env composition stays at call sites. Review argv tests still pass and spawner_test.go pins the new contract directly.\n- `validateRunID` is enforced at every state-store entry point (Save/Load/Clear), which closes the path-traversal door.\n- `adoptInvestigateEnv` correctly checks session/agent/starting-SHA AND short-circuits on `state.Kind != \"\"` for idempotency.\n- Per-turn `RunState.Save` after every advance means a Ctrl+C or crash leaves a recoverable state.\n- Test coverage of the resume path (`TestRunInvestigateLoop_Resume`) actually verifies the right agent runs first and quorum lands